course: dictionary
lesson: 9406
title: Squad: a group that works together
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context shows you which one is meant. «Squad» can mean a small group that works or trains together, the full group a coach picks a team from, or, in slang, your close friends.
explain: Look at the words around it. «The school squad trains every Tuesday» and «the coach picks the team from the squad» are about sport, so a squad is a group of players. «The bomb squad arrived in vans» is about police, so it means a special team trained for one job.
explain: Slang needs care too. «Me and my squad» means your close friends, and «squad goals» means a friend group that others want to be like. Choose the meaning that fits the clues, then check it by swapping it into the sentence.
fact: A squad is a small group of people who work or train together.
fact: In sport, the squad is the full group of players a coach can choose a team from.
fact: «The bomb squad» and «the drugs squad» are special teams of police, trained for one job.
fact: In slang, «my squad» means your close group of friends.
fact: Good teams share jobs, take turns, talk clearly and kindly, and laugh at the tea moments.
exercise: The coach picks the team from the squad. What is the squad here? | A kind of school bus | *The full group of players the coach can choose from | A tent for camping | A group of cooks in the school kitchen
exercise: «The bomb squad arrived in vans.» What does squad mean here? | A list of homework | *A special team of police trained for one job | A group of close friends | A football pitch
exercise: «Me and my squad went to the park.» What does squad mean? | *Your close group of friends | A team of soldiers at war | A school bus | A sports coach
test: «Zara plays on an old, slow laptop, so her squad picked games that run fine on old computers.» What does squad mean here? | A type of game | A shop that sells laptops | A computer part | *A group of friends who play together
test: Kai refuses to join random squads: «I don't know who they are, so no.» Why does he say no? | *Because he doesn't know who the people are, and he only plays with friends he knows in real life | Because he loves the tea moments | Because he only ever plays with friends he knows in real life | Because random squads always win
exam: Which meaning fits squad in «Ollie's squad thought he kept rage-quitting»? | The bus company | A cooking pot | *His group of friends who play games with him | A kind of wifi
